      {
        "id": 52781,
        "name": "jagarciao",
        "username": "jagarciao",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/jagarciao/{size}/2010_2.png",
        "created_at": "2013-02-06T22:30:34.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Sefirit\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/s/e68b1a/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Sefirit:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&nbsp;Then I saw Tempest and thought \"huh, I haven't ever had problems with him needing cards, but I guess more draw is always good.\"&nbsp; Still I when I play Tempest I rarely use that power, it seems more like a power that Expat would LOVE.&nbsp; It would help with card draw and bad draw issue and since she doen't have many one-shots that do damage she would basically pass a damage turn to get some juice.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>That sounds like a great idea… I might have to start working on a home-made alternate hero card for Expatriette.&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Restock Weapons</p>\n<p><b>Power:&nbsp;</b>Destroy 1 of your guns. If you do, draw 3 cards.&nbsp;</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>But to at least try to answer the OP's query, I do agree Unity is definitely gimped… but I have to admit that I like player her more now. I always felt she was a bit overpowered towards the end of the games before.&nbsp;</p>\n<p>I was definitely more concerned with Tempest's promo power, and I don't think I have seen it used more than once in games… and even then it was just to try it out.&nbsp;</p>\n<p>For what it's worth, I feel that if you really liked a character it's gonna be tough to get fully behind their promos since the whole point is to change their feel entirely and provide a different experience.</p>\n<p>I do like the fact that &gt;Games is willing to ruffle a few fan feathers to try new things with the game. And I love that the fan community is quick and loud in calling them out when they think something is off or unbalanced. It shows that this is a product people care about.&nbsp;</p>",
